<% content_for :title, "Impulsivity, Bipolar & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-impulsivity-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-hero">
    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-impulsivity-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / IMPULSIVITY
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>FUCK IT.</span>
        <span>I WANT IT</span>
        <span>RIGHT NOW.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-impulsivity-intro">
        THE DECISION TAKES A SECOND.<br>
        THE CONSEQUENCES CAN STICK AROUND.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / IMPULSIVITY -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-pink">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">01 / IMPULSIVITY</p>

      <h2>
        WANT IT.<br>
        DO IT.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        Sometimes the decision happens so quickly that you
        barely recognize it as a decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        You see something, want it, and act. Buy the thing.
        Order more shit. The immediate possibility takes up
        so much room that the consequences barely get a hearing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Later, you are left trying to understand how something
        that felt reasonable—or barely required thought—created
        another problem.
      </p>

      <div class="ai-cycle">
        <span>IMPULSE</span>
        <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
        <span>ACTION</span>
        <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
        <span>IMMEDIATE REWARD</span>
        <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
        <span>WHAT COMES AFTER</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        Impulsivity can also come after a long argument with
        yourself. You consider the cost, remember the consequences,
        and know what you intended to do. Then you get tired
        of considering it. Fuck it.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a moment, acting feels easier than continuing to think.
        Understanding that moment means paying attention to what
        makes you stop caring about information you already have.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        GIVE THE CONSEQUENCES<br>
        A CHANCE TO ENTER THE CONVERSATION.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / BIPOLAR -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-yellow">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">02 / BIPOLAR & YOUR OWN PATTERNS</p>

      <h2>
        WHEN<br>
        WAITING<br>
        FEELS POINTLESS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        Bipolar disorder belongs in this conversation because
        you know how different your judgment can feel during
        an episode.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking back at your manic episodes, you may recognize
        ideas arriving with an urgency and confidence that made
        waiting seem pointless. You wanted to do something,
        buy something, start something, or change everything
        at once.
      </p>

      <p>
        A purchase could feel necessary for the project you had
        just decided would transform your life. Concern from
        someone else could seem excessive because, from inside
        the experience, you felt certain.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your own patterns deserve more attention than a generic
        description of impulsivity. What changes first?
        Do you start browsing more, filling carts, making plans,
        or deciding that your usual limits no longer apply?
      </p>

      <p>
        When does spending stop feeling like a choice you are
        weighing and start feeling like something you need to do?
        Those details can help you recognize a familiar sequence
        before the consequences arrive.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        KNOW WHAT A CHANGE<br>
        LOOKS LIKE FOR YOU.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / SPENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-black">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">03 / SPENDING & ANTICIPATION</p>

      <h2>
        SOMETHING<br>
        TO LOOK<br>
        FORWARD TO.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        Shopping gives you something immediate: anticipation,
        novelty, and the feeling that something is happening.
      </p>

      <p>
        When pain limits your mobility and your options,
        a purchase can offer a small sense of movement.
        You can still choose something. You can still have
        something to look forward to.
      </p>

      <p>
        That appeal deserves an honest place in the account,
        especially when so much of your day involves restrictions.
      </p>

      <div class="ai-now-later">

        <div>
          <span>RIGHT NOW</span>
          <p>
            Anticipation. Novelty. Something to look forward to.
            A choice that feels like yours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <b>VS.</b>

        <div>
          <span>AFTERWARD</span>
          <p>
            The package arrives. The excitement changes.
            The money is still spent.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        You may genuinely enjoy what you bought while also
        recognizing that you needed those funds elsewhere.
        Both facts can be true.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking at the actual cost helps you decide what needs
        changing without pretending every purchase was meaningless
        or declaring yourself incapable of managing your life.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THE LARGER PATTERN -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-purple">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">04 / SUBSTANCE USE & THE LARGER PATTERN</p>

      <h2>
        LOOK<br>
        BEYOND<br>
        THE BOTTLE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        Being sober gives you room to examine impulsivity
        beyond drinking.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking back at substance use, consider where an immediate
        change in experience became more important than the larger
        decision. Sometimes the impulsive act was starting.
        Sometimes it was continuing beyond the limit you imagined.
      </p>

      <div class="ai-behaviors">

        <div>
          <span>ALCOHOL</span>
          <p>
            Look at where the decision to start—or continue—became
            disconnected from what you intended.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WEED</span>
          <p>
            Consider what changing your mental state offered
            and what consequences received less attention.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SHOPPING</span>
          <p>
            Notice when browsing becomes buying before you have
            checked the cost or considered whether you need it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>COMMITMENTS</span>
          <p>
            Enthusiasm can fill your schedule before you have
            considered your time, energy, pain, or existing plans.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>MESSAGES & DECISIONS</span>
          <p>
            Sending, agreeing, quitting, or changing a plan can
            happen before you have given yourself time
            to consider it.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        Look at where your intentions and your behavior have
        tended to separate. That is useful information for
        building safeguards around your own history.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not have to interpret every spontaneous action
        as a problem. Ask whether you had enough room to consider
        what you were doing and whether the action served
        the life you actually want.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / THE FUCK-ITS -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-pink">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">05 / THE FUCK-ITS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ARE<br>
        YOU FED<br>
        UP WITH?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        Sometimes the impulse carries a demand for freedom.
      </p>

      <p>
        The “fuck-its” are worth understanding closely.
        What are you fed up with? Pain? Boredom? Feeling
        dependent? Being responsible for every fucking
        detail of staying well?
      </p>

      <div class="ai-thoughts">
        <p>“I’M TIRED OF THINKING ABOUT EVERYTHING.”</p>
        <p>“I WANT SOMETHING TO LOOK FORWARD TO.”</p>
        <p>“I’LL DEAL WITH THE MONEY LATER.”</p>
        <p>“WHY CAN’T I JUST DO WHAT I WANT?”</p>
        <p>“FUCK IT. JUST THIS ONCE.”</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        You want a decision that belongs to you, without
        another restriction or consequence to consider.
        Ask what kind of freedom the action provides and
        what it leaves you dealing with afterward.
      </p>

      <p>
        There may be a part of you that is tired of always
        being the person who has to think ahead. Listen to it.
        What would make ordinary life less exhausting?
        Where could you have more enjoyment, choice, or help?
      </p>

      <p>
        You can take those needs seriously while keeping
        essential money, sobriety, and other commitments protected.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / TIME AND INFORMATION -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-yellow">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">06 / TIME & INFORMATION</p>

      <h2>
        GIVE IT<br>
        SOME FUCKING<br>
        TIME.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        A pause can help when you have enough room to use one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Give the decision an actual time to return, so postponing
        it does not become an endless argument in your head.
        When you revisit it, consider the cost, the reason,
        and what else the decision affects.
      </p>

      <div class="ai-friction-grid">

        <div>
          <span>THE CART</span>
          <p>
            Save the item without checking out.
            Give yourself a specific time to reconsider it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>THE MONEY</span>
          <p>
            Check what is available after essentials
            and existing commitments.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>THE MESSAGE</span>
          <p>
            Save the draft. Read it again when you have
            more room to consider what you want to say.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>THE COMMITMENT</span>
          <p>
            Check your schedule, energy, and physical limits
            before giving an answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>THE SUPPORT</span>
          <p>
            Decide which purchases or major decisions
            you want to discuss with someone first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>THE REASON</span>
          <p>
            Ask what appeals to you and whether the decision
            still makes sense after the initial excitement.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        For spending, make the numbers visible. What is available
        after essentials? What have you already committed to?
        What would buying this mean for the rest of the month?
      </p>

      <p>
        An accurate budget gives you something concrete to consult
        when enthusiasm starts making everything sound affordable.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        GIVE YOURSELF TIME<br>
        TO CONSIDER THE WHOLE DECISION.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / SUPPORT -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-purple">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">07 / SUPPORT & SAFEGUARDS</p>

      <h2>
        MAKE<br>
        THE PLAN<br>
        TOGETHER.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        Some periods require more support than a pause
        you are expected to remember on your own.
      </p>

      <p>
        You already take your medication. You can still need
        practical safeguards and help recognizing changes.
        Planning with trusted people and your treatment team
        gives you a way to respond when your judgment becomes
        harder to assess from inside the experience.
      </p>

      <div class="ai-pause">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>KNOW YOUR PATTERNS.</strong><br>
            Identify changes in spending, sleep, energy,
            confidence, and behavior that deserve attention.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>AGREE ON THE SUPPORT.</strong><br>
            Discuss what help you would accept
            and how someone should raise a concern.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>PROTECT ESSENTIALS.</strong><br>
            Arrange practical safeguards for money
            and commitments before you need them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>INVOLVE YOUR TREATMENT TEAM.</strong><br>
            Communicate meaningful changes rather than
            expecting yourself to interpret everything alone.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        Make those arrangements specific while you have room
        to consider them. What changes should someone mention?
        What help would you accept with spending?
      </p>

      <p>
        Who needs to know if your sleep, energy, confidence,
        or behavior starts shifting? A plan is easier to use
        when everyone understands what the support is supposed
        to look like.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / ACCOUNTABILITY -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-section ai-black">

    <div class="addiction-impulsivity-heading">
      <p class="ai-number">08 / ACCOUNTABILITY & LEARNING</p>

      <h2>
        FACE IT.<br>
        REPAIR IT.<br>
        LEARN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ai-copy">

      <p class="ai-lead">
        You also have responsibilities after an impulsive decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        Check whether something can be returned. Address the payment.
        Acknowledge the commitment you cannot keep. Apologize when
        your action affected someone else.
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding your mental state helps explain what support
        was needed; the practical consequences still deserve attention.
      </p>

      <p>
        Try to keep that review specific. “I spent money reserved
        for a bill” identifies a problem you can work on.
        “I’m a fucking disaster” can occupy an entire evening
        without helping you pay anything.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what happened, where you had room to intervene,
        and where you needed more support than you had.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can learn from an impulsive action without repeatedly
        humiliating yourself over it. Shame may make you avoid
        the account, hide the purchase, or disappear from
        a conversation you need to have.
      </p>

      <p>
        Stay close enough to the facts to do something useful.
        Taking responsibility includes being willing to look.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        OWN WHAT HAPPENED.<br>
        GIVE YOURSELF SOMETHING USEFUL TO DO NEXT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-impulsivity-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="ai-ending-small">
        MORE TIME. MORE INFORMATION. MORE SUPPORT.
      </p>

      <h2>
        GIVE<br>
        YOURSELF<br>
        <span>ROOM.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ai-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Your creativity, enthusiasm, and desire for an interesting
        life deserve room too. You can have new ideas, start projects,
        enjoy things, and occasionally make a spontaneous decision.
        Give yourself enough structure that those experiences have
        a place alongside the commitments you want to keep.
      </p>

      <p>
        Over time, look for changes in how you participate.
        You notice a familiar pattern sooner. You leave something
        in the cart. You ask for another opinion. You admit that
        your judgment feels different. You deal with a mistake
        before avoidance makes it larger.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those are concrete ways of creating more room between
        what you feel and what you do.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not have to become someone who never wants anything
        urgently. Keep learning what urgency does to your judgment,
        what your own patterns look like, and what helps you make
        a decision you can stand behind later.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your next choice deserves enough time, information,
        and support to belong to more than the feeling
        of the moment.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "ai-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>